/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122790" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "291ee631bf62fbaa8e8842bd258b564692b21cc9a695ab81dd4f261b64e27ac2"} {"question" "q-122790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/00b722a09fef4428d17fc7ff6a301b3860e798b20c44126b63cd381ad2884398.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/04f52ccb3eed1f0377edebf34607812f2c8eefe27e910e250959e8fa39ef13d4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/9b8e151985327da40d1fb3044c7f4c785a9f0fcbaa5d71d7f865d4b3fee38ab9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/ae9e6dc29361d66784dae2868e500940132049cfa04b65aafddc06d35c1afdbf.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=-1,$ which gives the vertical hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the pair of lines $y=\\pm x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2,$ which gives the horizontal hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the right half-plane $x>0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the left half-plane $x<0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/037f46d3fdd190749c2aefe51222475fdd337a7b3ce53e6809048e3491a31a47.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "291ee631bf62fbaa8e8842bd258b564692b21cc9a695ab81dd4f261b64e27ac2"} {"question" "q-122834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/08a6451e354eaaad23ef233a8c43d16512a2ed33257cd9e9b1257ef5eaaf558b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refe