athacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/e6598c50e72417a0bff5514fa2b8ff9fba7c617cff10dc83e15472259d040846.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122796" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "291ee631bf62fbaa8e8842bd258b564692b21cc9a695ab81dd4f261b64e27ac2"} {"question" "q-122572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/229bb0ca171a2f77f16e15471bce4037e642299e7141025bfb178a81f901ee02.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/372482df951dbb50d6db837939a0708a9a69ea330c03e59bce19398575ad50f7.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/c3f79f33b0a983f7c92acebaddbd3bc5208275cca585ac5464ddf44c9a7e8d87.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/d55feb791b51d0b4ebfe6b8998f1aad1e1a8d57d1593097b0c99d706a01d47a8.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "q-122572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/state.json" "file_sha256" "9616a225ec7db72d7066c66de8a1367f4d7f6b8d1bec970713249a932014c85b"} {"question" "e-9453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = e^{2x-3y}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/example-9453.json" "file_sha256" "755794c860ae212971fe7dc329bfbbbd9632d4fc5ebc6fb54742ee553a84cd70"} {"question" "e-9453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y}\\,i-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/example-9453.json" "file_sha256" "755794c860ae212971fe7dc329bfbbbd9632d4fc5ebc6fb54742ee553a84cd70"} {"question" "e-9474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/f9de4a1c2dfb6b8939b2440930eefb8b7020947bd7b943021214adf4cbfce735.png)\n\n    \n\nWhich of the above diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = 2y^2-x^2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/example-9474.json" "file_sha256" "796ae4c29efb3e896ae13968248722890658cbd0b5cab4c62d0ff71677dfa9b8"} {"question" "e-9474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2x\\,i+4y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(-2,1),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =-2\\cdot 2\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,1) & =-2\\cdot (-2)\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\,i+4\\cdot (-1)\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/b88c62b154cecaace206248b62f72b8aa1f71662c0427adfbbabd5c2b4738a6b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/example-9474.json" "file_sha256" "796ae4c29efb3e896ae13968248722890658cbd0b5cab4c62d0ff71677dfa9b8"} {"question" "e-9444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/c4d5f211c3bf3d306c6dc14205311c60eeaaf55de89f8af571ec0fa9e5add8a6.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/example-9444.json" "file_sha256" "7417dcd9eae8a03b1c13e585c39b67874133470056e5e2386961302080c3caa7"} {"question" "e-9444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1,$ we get $xy = -1,$ which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0,$ we get $xy = 0,$ that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1,$ we get $xy =1,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2,$ we get $xy =-2,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/f95c7ab30b7b02d67ceec039792e649a900dbece2896cc1e63341fcba9fc794f.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/3d6be27e083bb2e8b508cca31671e5cb4ea4e39b366a99f1dd194a5ea8ffcd3b.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/996b5678396b4e9a91a4c55697442895331a7a6e4f8ec3eed550ea86845e44f4.png)\n\n       \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-c