4y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( x , y ) & = {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = - 2 x \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 y \\, \\mathbf{j} .\\end{array}$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1)$, $(-2,1)$, and $(1,-1)$.\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f$.\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 2 , 1 ) & = - 2 \\cdot  2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\cdot  1 \\, \\mathbf{j} = - 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( - 2 , 1 ) & = - 2 \\cdot  ( - 2 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\cdot  1 \\, \\mathbf{j} = 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 1 , - 1 ) & = - 2 \\cdot  1 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\cdot  ( - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{j} = - 2 \\, \\mathbf{i} - 4 \\, \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/5cb2c61d730527746c2d42d5a8af642e.png)"] [17592186179504 :question/problem "Which of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/87979b85b4ea12aacf6bd158adc3fcb2.png)"] [17592186179504 :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1$, we get $xy = -1$, which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0$, we get $xy = 0$, that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1$, we get $xy =1$, that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2$, we get $xy =-2$, that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/5691898f126e352c214c8c48bb94c312.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0$, and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0$.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/54fea7979eb64dee5d535a276bd80c43.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/55af8d7d2226cd79132c79e19bbce747.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186177377 17592186177378 17592186177379 17592186177380 17592186177381 17592186177413 17592186177414 17592186177415 17592186177416 17592186177417 17592186177425 17592186177426 17592186177427 17592186177428 17592186177429] "immutable_fields" [[17592186177376 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177376 :answer-field/id] [17592186177376 :answer-field/key] [17592186177376 :answer-field/type] [17592186177412 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177412 :answer-field/id] [17592186177412 :answer-field/key] [17592186177412 :answer-field/type] [17592186177424 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177424 :answer-field/id] [17592186177424 :answer-field/key] [17592186177424 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
